WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:05.140
The Nightmare Before Christmas is a beloved
classic, one of the most singularly quirky

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:10.120
and amazing animated films, holiday movies,
and musicals of all time, all rolled into

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:10.920
one.

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:15.120
Coming from the mind of producer Tim Burton
and bearing his distinct visual stamp, the

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:20.730
stop-motion animated feature has become a
staple of the holidays since its 1993 release.

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:24.731
One of the true joys of The Nightmare Before
Christmas is how amazingly true it is to the

00:00:24.731 --> 00:00:29.769
tone and spirit of both Halloween and Christmas,
and it's easy to see why the film has become

00:00:29.769 --> 00:00:32.060
a yearly tradition for so many.

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:36.190
But considering its financial and critical
success and its impact on the world of pop

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:40.879
culture, what's not so easy to puzzle out
is why we never got a sequel to the classic

00:00:40.879 --> 00:00:41.879
film.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:44.640
Why did we never get The Nightmare Before
Christmas 2?

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.200
"Terrible news, folks!

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:51.179
The worst tragedy of our times!"

00:00:51.179 --> 00:00:53.440
It's not like the idea hasn't been kicked
around.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:57.899
In particular, the film's director Henry Selick
- who would go on to work on two additional

00:00:57.899 --> 00:01:03.809
stop-motion features, 2006's James and the
Giant Peach and 2009's Coraline - has gone

00:01:03.809 --> 00:01:07.770
on record stating that while he could be enticed
to return to Halloween Town.

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:10.170
But the notion was complicated by Disney.

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:14.010
It seems that the House of Mouse was only
interested in a sequel to the classic on one

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:17.620
specific condition - one that Selick wasn't
too keen on.

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:22.250
In a 2009 interview, Selick revealed that
discussions about a sequel had in fact taken

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:23.250
place, saying,

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:27.840
"A few years back, Disney spoke to me and
the sad thing was at the time, they said,

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.340
'If we do a sequel, it will have to be CG.'

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:31.670
I was really disappointed.

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:36.660
I asked why, and they didn't think stop-motion
was a viable way to make movies [...] I don't

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:39.200
think Tim would allow a CG sequel.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:43.990
There's been a few stories proposed and a
few discussions, but that's really Tim's decision."

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:48.330
In sharing this, Selick hit upon one major
roadblock to any sequel to The Nightmare Before

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:53.050
Christmas: Burton, who first conceived the
story and characters in a poem he wrote as

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:54.680
a young man in the early '80s.

00:01:54.680 --> 00:02:00.610
In a 2006 interview with MTV News, the filmmaker
indicated that he may not be terribly open

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:04.280
to the idea of any sequel, CG or otherwise.

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:05.280
He told MTV,

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:10.280
"I was always very protective of [the movie],
not to do sequels or things of that kind.

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:14.590
You know, 'Jack visits Thanksgiving world'
or other kinds of things, just because I felt

00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:17.780
the movie had a purity to it and the people
that like it.

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:21.720
Because it's not a mass-market kind of thing,
it was important to kind of keep that purity

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:22.720
of it."

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:26.560
That's certainly understandable, but even
if some persistent executive had been able

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:31.000
to convince Burton to make The Nightmare Before
Christmas 2 any way he wanted, there were

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:35.300
many years during which it just wouldn't have
been possible - because the filmmaker wasn't

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:39.440
speaking to his star, Danny Elfman, who provided
Jack's singing voice.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:43.520
In case you're not familiar with Elfman, you
should know that you are, in fact, familiar

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:44.540
with Elfman.

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:49.190
The former front man for new wave pioneers
Oingo Boingo, Elfman has composed so much

00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:54.100
music for film and television - including
the iconic Simpsons theme - that it'd be virtually

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:57.190
impossible to have never heard any of his
work.

00:02:57.190 --> 00:03:01.830
Elfman has worked with Burton on no fewer
than 16 films, and during the course of their

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:06.959
long professional relationship, the two famously
eccentric personalities didn't always play

00:03:06.959 --> 00:03:08.190
nice together.

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:12.440
In a 2015 interview, Elfman revealed that
the pair had a big blow up right after work

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:16.650
on The Nightmare Before Christmas was completed
- one which would have kept the musical voice

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:19.920
of Jack from participating in any sequel for
some time.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:20.920
Elfman recalled,

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:25.590
"It was in the middle of a very intense period
[...] and I think things came to a boil.

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:29.750
It's inevitable that in over a quarter of
a century, two personalities like us - and

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:34.150
I can't pretend that I'm not volatile and
was more so then - there's gotta be some kind

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:35.170
of explosion."

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:39.230
The pair resolved their differences several
years later, but they did lose a lot of time

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:42.690
during which The Nightmare Before Christmas
was fresh in the minds of the movie-going

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:43.690
public.

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:47.930
To be sure, the original film would be a tough
act to follow, even assuming that Burton,

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:50.870
Elfman, and Selick could be coaxed back on
board.

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:54.720
You may have heard, however, that the House
of Mouse has recently been diving into its

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:59.459
archives in pursuit of box office gold - meaning
that if we ever do get more Nightmare Before

00:03:59.459 --> 00:04:03.390
Christmas, it may not necessarily be in the
form of a sequel.

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:07.180
Disney's recent live-action remakes of its
classic animated properties have, for the

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:09.910
most part, done bonkers box office.

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:15.209
Interestingly, the sole exception is 2019's
Dumbo, a comparative flop that just happened

00:04:15.209 --> 00:04:18.909
to have been directed by Tim Burton - not
that this would keep Disney from eyeing The

00:04:18.909 --> 00:04:21.819
Nightmare Before Christmas for the live-action
treatment.

00:04:21.819 --> 00:04:26.240
In fact, it's recently been rumored that the
studio is considering doing just that - and

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:31.050
these rumors surfaced early in 2019, before
Aladdin and The Lion King each scored over

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:33.960
a billion dollars at the worldwide box office.

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:38.389
With Mulan, The Little Mermaid, and more looking
to continue the trend, it stands to reason

00:04:38.389 --> 00:04:42.539
that the Mouse House might be taking a hard
look at their stop-motion holiday classic

00:04:42.539 --> 00:04:44.909
for possible live-action adaptation.

00:04:44.909 --> 00:04:49.360
Of course, it should be stressed that these
are merely rumors and are in no way confirmed

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:54.310
- but at this point, a live-action redo seems
a lot more likely than a stop-motion sequel.

00:04:54.310 --> 00:04:58.529
Honestly, we'll take either one - just for
the chance to see Jack the Pumpkin King back

00:04:58.529 --> 00:05:00.580
up on the big screen one more time.

